ke, simple, 128s` | [ ] |\n| 32 | all | `shake, simple, 128f` | [ ] |\n| 33 | all | `shake, simple, 192s` | [ ] |\n| 34 | all | `shake, simple, 192f` | [ ] |\n| 35 | all | `shake, simple, 256s` | [ ] |\n| 36 | all | `shake, simple, 256f` | [ ] |\n| 37 | all | `blake, robust, 128s` (BLAKE-256 thash path) | [ ] |\n| 38 | all | `blake, robust, 128f` (BLAKE-256 thash path) | [ ] |\n| 39 | all | `blake, robust, 192s` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 40 | all | `blake, robust, 192f` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 41 | all | `blake, robust, 256s` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 42 | all | `blake, robust, 256f` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 43 | all | `blake, simple, 128s` (BLAKE-256 thash path) | [ ] |\n| 44 | all | `blake, simple, 128f` (BLAKE-256 thash path) | [ ] |\n| 45 | all | `blake, simple, 192s` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 46 | all | `blake, simple, 192f` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 47 | all | `blake, simple, 256s` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n| 48 | all | `blake, simple, 256f` (BLAKE-512 for multi-block thash) | [ ] |\n\nParameter shapes are: `128s=(N=16,D=7,tree_height=9,FORS=12x14)`,\n`128f=(16,22,3,6x33)`, `192s=(24,7,9,14x17)`,\n`192f=(24,22,3,8x33)`, `256s=(32,8,8,14x22)`, and\n`256f=(32,17,4,9x35)`.\n\n## Runtime/data-shape combinations\n\nThese rows are run under every build-time combination when the named backend\nentry point exists.\n\n| # | entry point(s) | configuration (options set + input shape) | [ ] |\n|---:|---|---|:---:|\n| 49 | four `crypto_sign_*bytes` queries | no input; compare all parameter-derived sizes | [ ] |\n| 50 | `SPX_ull_to_bytes`, `SPX_bytes_to_ull` | widths 0, 1, 2, 4, and 8; randomized values; big-endian truncation | [ ] |\n| 51 | `SPX_u32_to_bytes` | randomized 32-bit values including 0 and `UINT32_MAX` | [ ] |\n| 52 | all eight address setters | zero, in-range, and high-bit values; byte-truncating and full-width fields | [ ] |\n| 53 | both address copy functions | randomized source/destination bytes, including overlap-preserved fields | [ ] |\n| 54 | backend initialize/PRF | randomized public/secret seeds and all seven address types | [ ] |\n| 55 | backend message-random function | message lengths 0, 1, block-1, block, block+1, and multi-block | [ ] |\n| 56 | backend message hash | same message shapes; verify digest, masked tree index, and masked leaf index | [ ] |\n| 57 | `SPX_thash` | `inblocks=1` (F function) | [ ] |\n| 58 | `SPX_thash` | `inblocks=2` (Merkle/FORS internal node; wide hash when `N>=24`) | [ ] |\n| 59 | `SPX_thash` | `inblocks=SPX_WOTS_LEN` | [ ] |\n| 60 | `SPX_thash` | `inblocks=SPX_FORS_TREES` | [ ] |\n| 61 | `SPX_chain_lengths` | randomized `SPX_N`-byte messages, including all-zero/all-`0xff` | [ ] |\n| 62 | `SPX_wots_pk_from_sig` | randomized WOTS signature/message/context/address | [ ] |\n| 63 | `SPX_compute_root` | even leaf, nonzero offset, every valid tree height used by the parameter set | [ ] |\n| 64 | `SPX_compute_root` | odd leaf, zero/nonzero offset, every valid tree height used by the parameter set | [ ] |\n| 65 | `SPX_treehash` | direct callback API, leaf indices at first/middle/last and nonzero offsets | [ ] |\n| 66 | `SPX_wots_gen_leafx1` | generated leaf is not the signing leaf | [ ] |\n| 67 | `SPX_wots_gen_leafx1` | generated leaf is the signing leaf; capture WOTS signature steps | [ ] |\n| 68 | `SPX_wots_treehashx1` | first/middle/last signing leaf and authentication path | [ ] |\n| 69 | `SPX_fors_gen_leafx1` | randomized FORS address/index | [ ] |\n| 70 | `SPX_fors_treehashx1` | first/middle/last FORS leaf and nonzero tree offset | [ ] |\n| 71 | `SPX_fors_sign`, `SPX_fors_pk_from_sig` | randomized message digest/context/address; compare signature and recovered PK | [ ] |\n| 72 | `SPX_merkle_sign` | first/middle/last leaf; compare WOTS signature, auth path, and root | [ ] |\n| 73 | `SPX_merkle_gen_root` | randomized context seeds | [ ] |\n| 74 | `crypto_sign_seed_keypair` | randomized fixed-size seeds | [ ] |\n| 75 | `crypto_sign_signature`, `crypto_sign_verify` | deterministic RNG initialized identically; empty, one-byte, and multi-block messages | [ ] |\n| 76 | `crypto_sign`, `crypto_sign_open` | deterministic RNG initialized identically; disjoint and overlapping message/output buffers | [ ] |\n| 77 | BLAKE one-shot functions | lengths 0, 1, 55/56, 63/64/65 and 111/112, 127/128/129 | [ ] |\n| 78 | BLAKE incremental functions | zero/one/many updates crossing 64- and 128-byte compression boundaries | [ ] |\n| 79 | BLAKE MGF1 functions | output lengths 0, 1, digest-1, digest, digest+1, and multiple digests | [ ] |\n| 80 | SHA-2 one-shot/incremental functions | empty, padding boundaries, one/many full blocks, and remainder finalization | [ ] |\n| 81 | SHA-2 MGF1/state seeding | short/exact/long outputs and both `N<24`/`N>=24` branches | [ ] |\n| 82 | SHAKE one-shot/incremental functions | empty, rate-1, rate, rate+1, and multi-rate absorb/squeeze | [ ] |\n| 83 | Haraka permutation/hash functions | 32-byte, 64-byte, incremental sponge, and partial squeeze shapes | [ ] |\n| 84 | deterministic RNG/AES functions | personalization absent/present; requests 0, 1, 15, 16, 17, and many blocks | [ ] |\n# Error-surface table\n\nThis table comes from mechanical searches for negative/sentinel returns,\nnull/range checks, error constants, aborts, and compile-time `#error` guards.\nRows 1–7 are input-triggerable API rejections. Rows 8–10 are internal OpenSSL\nfatal paths in the deterministic RNG implementation.\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result |\n|---:|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `crypto_sign_verify` | `siglen != SPX_BYTES` | return `-1` before reading the signature |\n| 2 | `crypto_sign_verify` | reconstructed root differs from `pk + SPX_N` (`memcmp(...) != 0`) | return `-1` |\n| 3 | `crypto_sign_open` | `smlen < SPX_BYTES` | zero exactly `smlen` output bytes, set `*mlen = 0`, return `-1` |\n| 4 | `crypto_sign_open` | embedded `crypto_sign_verify(...)` returns nonzero | zero exactly `smlen` output bytes, set `*mlen = 0`, return `-1` |\n| 5 | `seedexpander_init` | `maxlen >= 0x100000000` | return `RNG_BAD_MAXLEN` (`-1`) |\n| 6 | `seedexpander` | output pointer `x == NULL` | return `RNG_BAD_OUTBUF` (`-2`) |\n| 7 | `seedexpander` | `xlen >= ctx->length_remaining` (equality is rejected) | return `RNG_BAD_REQ_LEN` (`-3`) without consuming bytes |\n| 8 | `AES256_ECB` | `EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new()` returns `NULL` | print OpenSSL errors and abort |\n| 9 | `AES256_ECB` | `EVP_EncryptInit_ex(...) != 1` | print OpenSSL errors and abort |\n| 10 | `AES256_ECB` | `EVP_EncryptUpdate(...) != 1` | print OpenSSL errors and abort |\n\nNo C `assert` statements, `RETURN_ERROR` macros, `return NULL` statements, or\npublic enum-valued inputs exist in this source tree.\n\nBuild-time guards (not FFI inputs) reject: a BLAKE/SHA-256 output shorter than\n`SPX_N`; subtree addressing needing more than 64 bits; message tree indices\nneeding more than 64 bits; SHA input parameters outside the implementation's\nsupported block-size assumptions.\nfatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git\n","exit_code":128,"status":"failed"}}
